= 1F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=1F}

Put $q=(1,1,1,1)^{\mathsf T}$ and arrange the four vectors as the columns of $A=(x-1)I+qq^{\mathsf T}$. On the one-dimensional <vector subspace> spanned by $q$, the <eigenvalue> is $x+3$. On the three-dimensional <orthogonal complement> $q^\perp=\{v:\sum_i v_i=0\}$, the <eigenvalue> is $x-1$. Thus
$$
\det A=(x+3)(x-1)^3,
$$
and **the vectors fail to be a basis exactly when $x=1$ or $x=-3$.**

For $x=1$, all four columns equal $q$. The <matrix rank> is $1$, a <basis> of their <span> is $\{q\}$, and an extension to a <basis> of $\mathbb R^4$ is
$$
\boxed{\{q,e_1,e_2,e_3\}.}
$$
The last coordinate first forces the coefficient of $q$ to vanish in any <linear dependence>, after which the other coefficients vanish.

For $x=-3$, the <matrix rank> is $3$ and the <span> is exactly $q^\perp$. A <basis> consists of the first three original columns,
$$
\boxed{\{(-3,1,1,1),(1,-3,1,1),(1,1,-3,1)\}.}
$$
Indeed, writing them as $q-4e_i$, a vanishing linear combination has coefficient sum zero by its fourth coordinate, and then each coefficient is zero by the first three coordinates. Adjoin $q$ to extend this <basis> to $\mathbb R^4$, since $q\notin q^\perp$.

= 2E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Groups, Rings and Modules}

= Solution
{parent=2E}

**Eisenstein's criterion.** Let $f(X)=a_nX^n+\cdots+a_0\in\mathbb Z[X]$ be a nonconstant <primitive polynomial>. If a <prime number> $p$ satisfies $p\nmid a_n$, $p\mid a_j$ for $0\leq j<n$, and $p^2\nmid a_0$, then $f$ is an <irreducible polynomial> over $\mathbb Q$.

To prove the <Eisenstein criterion>, suppose a nontrivial factorization exists over $\mathbb Q$. <Gauss lemma for polynomials> gives $f=gh$ with $g,h\in\mathbb Z[X]$ of positive degree. Their leading coefficients are not divisible by $p$, because their product is $a_n$. Reduction modulo $p$ therefore preserves both degrees, and
$$
\overline g\,\overline h=\overline{a_n}X^n\quad\text{in }\mathbb F_p[X].
$$
Since the only irreducible factor of the right side is $X$, both reduced factors are nonzero monomials of positive degree. Their constant coefficients are consequently divisible by $p$. This would make $p^2\mid g(0)h(0)=a_0$, a contradiction.

For the requested <polynomial>, shift the variable by one. The <binomial theorem> gives
$$
\Phi_p(X+1)=\frac{(X+1)^p-1}{X}=\sum_{j=1}^p\binom pjX^{j-1}.
$$
It is monic, all coefficients below the leading one are divisible by $p$, and the constant coefficient is $p$, not divisible by $p^2$. The <Eisenstein criterion> proves irreducibility of this shifted <polynomial>. The substitution $X\mapsto X+1$ is an <automorphism> of $\mathbb Q[X]$, with inverse $X\mapsto X-1$, so it preserves nontrivial factorizations. Hence
$$
\boxed{X^{p-1}+X^{p-2}+\cdots+1\text{ is irreducible over }\mathbb Q.}
$$
This is the <cyclotomic polynomial> for a prime index.

= 3G
